---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
const title = "Sources & editorial standards";
const description = "How to distinguish original experiments, implementation reports, agent-assisted references and mirrored sources on LLMSX.";
---
<Base title={title} description={description} route="/editorial/">
  <p class="lede">Read a claim at the level its evidence supports.</p>
  <p>LLMSX combines original project writing, agent-assisted research and third-party reference material. Those sources have different limits. A format score, a citation and a measured result are not interchangeable.</p>
  <h2>Original guides and research</h2>
  <p>An experiment should name its questions, environment, comparison and observed results. Readers should be able to inspect the underlying artifacts. A result from two repositories or one model does not establish how every system behaves. Negative results and failed setups matter as much as improvements.</p>
  <p>An implementation or incident report describes a particular system and what happened there. An architecture paper explains a design; it does not demonstrate an effect size unless it includes a measurement. Publication dates identify when an article was published. A date in the body may identify an older implementation or dataset.</p>
  <p>For an example with public methods and raw results, see the <a href="/blog/what-docs-llms-files-and-indexes-actually-save/">documentation cost experiment</a>. For a design report that states its measurement gaps, see <a href="/blog/state-that-survives-the-session/">State That Survives the Session</a>.</p>
  <h2>Agent-assisted references</h2>
  <p>Concept references organize material into summaries, facets and source-linked facts. Some were assembled with AI-assisted research and extraction. Their source links can point to an upstream document or to a report mirrored on this site. Follow that report's own references when you need the primary evidence.</p>
  <p>A source link makes a claim inspectable. It does not establish that the source is authoritative, current or correctly represented. Check version-sensitive commands and technical limits against current primary documentation before applying them.</p>
  <h2>Mirrors and scores</h2>
  <p>Pages under <code>/sources/</code> preserve reports used by the library. They provide provenance and access, rather than a separate claim of original reporting. The <a href="/directory/">source directory</a> adds format analysis to llms files. Its grades assess the stated <a href="/reference/">rubric</a>; they do not certify factual accuracy.</p>
  <h2>Incomplete work and corrections</h2>
  <p>Topic entries without a published reference remain browsable as part of the map. They are labelled as topic entries and excluded from Search. That exclusion is a content availability rule, not evidence that the remaining references passed a fact check.</p>
  <p>If an article's public evidence does not support its measured-result framing, flag that gap prominently and exclude it from promotion and Search until the evidence is resolved. Do not silently turn an unsupported measurement into an estimate.</p>
  <p>Report a correction with a page URL and the specific evidence through <a href="https://github.com/mithudso/llms-explorer/issues">GitHub issues</a> or the <a href="/about/#contact">contact address</a>. Keep the original context available when documenting a correction so readers can understand what changed.</p>
  <h2>AI assistance and reader judgment</h2>
  <p>AI assistance is part of this project, including research and writing. It does not replace source inspection or reproduce an experiment by itself. Treat examples as examples, architecture as architecture, and only supported observations as measurements.</p>
</Base>
